The L'Oiseau Blanc was a Stinson SM-6000 aircraft that attempted to fly across the Atlantic Ocean in 1933. It was piloted by two French aviators, Henri Guillaumet and Maurice Rossi, but unfortunately, they disappeared during the flight. The significance of the L'Oiseau Blanc lies in its attempt to be the first aircraft to fly non-stop from Europe to North America, which was a major milestone in the history of aviation.
