Based on my internal knowledge, I can provide the following information:

Alfred Gell was a British social anthropologist. He was born in 1944 in London, England. Gell's notable contributions to the field of social anthropology include his work on art and agency, as well as his development of the "art as agency" theory. He is also known for his book "Art and Agency: An Anthropological Theory" (1998), which explores the relationship between art and the people who create it.

Edmund Leach was a British social anthropologist. He was born in 1910 in London, England. Leach's notable contributions to the field of social anthropology include his work on kinship and marriage, as well as his development of the "structural functionalism" theory. He is also known for his book "Political Systems of Highland Burma: A Study of Kachin Social Structure" (1954), which examines the social structure of the Kachin people in Burma (now Myanmar).

Both Gell and Leach were influential figures in the field of social anthropology, and their work continues to be studied and debated by scholars today.
