Explain the context and significance of the Rostker v. Goldberg case in relation to the US armed forces' personnel selection process.
The Rostker v. Goldberg case was a landmark decision by the United States Supreme Court in 1981 that held the practice of requiring only men to register for the draft was constitutional. The case centered on the Selective Service System's (MSSA) policy of only requiring men to register for the draft, while women were exempt. The plaintiffs, including Robert L. Goldberg, challenged this policy as unconstitutional, arguing it was discriminatory.

The significance of the Rostker v. Goldberg case lies in its impact on the US armed forces' personnel selection process. The decision upheld the MSSA's policy of only requiring men to register for the draft, which meant that women were not subject to the draft and were not required to register. This decision was significant because it reinforced the existing policy of excluding women from combat roles in the military, as mentioned in the context, and solidified the notion that women were not subject to the same military service obligations as men.

In essence, the Rostker v. Goldberg case reinforced the existing gender-based distinction in the US armed forces' personnel selection process, where women were not required to register for the draft or serve in combat roles. This decision had far-reaching implications for the military's personnel policies and the role of women in the armed forces.
