Identify the historical Western Germanic language spoken in the region where the settlement on the river Leda opposite Leer is located, and determine its time period of usage.
Based on the context, the settlement on the river Leda opposite Leer is located in the "Overledingen Land" (Old Frisian "mutha" meaning "(river) mouth"). The West Germanic language spoken in this region is likely to be an Ingvaeonic (North Sea Germanic) language, specifically an Old Frisian language.

Old Frisian was spoken in the region of Frisia, which includes the area around the river Leda, from the 5th to the 15th century. It is the ancestor of the modern Frisian languages, such as West Frisian, East Frisian, and North Frisian.

Therefore, the answer is: Old Frisian, from the 5th to the 15th century.
